FINDINGS = [
 ("F1", "Background", "Chapter 14 of the 3rd edition teaches how a Python program reads and writes Excel workbooks with the third-party module openpyxl: it opens a workbook and takes sheets and cells from it, converts between column letters and numbers, reads rows and columns, builds two projects on real data (a census tally over 72,865 tract rows in Project 9 and a price correction over 23,758 produce rows in Project 10), creates and removes sheets, writes values and formulas, sets fonts, row heights and column widths, merges cells, freezes panes and draws charts, and ends with 18 practice questions and two practice programs (a multiplication table and a blank row inserter) [P01] [P02]. The chapter was fetched on 2026-10-12 and its sha256 equals the digest in the book's own ontology; it has 36 listings and 12 figures.", ["P01", "P02", "P41", "P42"]),
 ("F2", "Comparative analysis", "The printed behaviour of the chapter reproduces wherever it can run: under Python 3.14.6 with openpyxl 3.1.5 the shell sessions of the chapter gave the printed values (the sheet names, the datetime of A1, 'Apples', the column letters 'A', 'B', 'AA' and 'AHP', the slice of cells, the font and merge sessions), Project 9 run whole on the real census file printed Opening workbook, Reading rows, Writing results and Done and left {'pop': 291826, 'tracts': 55} for Anchorage, and Project 10 run whole on the real produce file changed Garlic in 630 rows, Celery in 586 and Lemon in 607 [P01] [P03] [P04]. The one openpyxl program recorded under 3.14.0 to 3.14.6, 3.13 and 3.12 printed the same bodies everywhere, with no warning even when warnings were turned into errors.", ["P01", "P03", "P04", "P19", "P40"]),
 ("F3", "Comparative analysis", "The two Python scripts that the book's online materials ship with the files are in the style of the 2nd edition and do not run today: readCensusExcel.py calls wb.get_sheet_by_name, which only draws a DeprecationWarning, and sheet.get_highest_row(), which no longer exists, so it prints Opening workbook and Reading rows and then stops with AttributeError, and updateProduce.py opens a file named produceSales.xlsx that is not in the zip (the file is produceSales3.xlsx), and its loop range(2, get_highest_row()) would leave the last row out [P41] [P03]. The third script, census2010.py, is the result of Project 9: the one the chapter's listing generates equals it except that the shipped copy has Windows line endings (3,142 CRLF; 155,379 bytes against 152,237). The files at autbor.com and automatetheboringstuff.com/files for the 3rd edition answered 404 for produceSales3.xlsx.", ["P41", "P03", "P04", "P01"]),
 ("F4", "Comparative analysis", "The chapter states the order of the arguments of Reference the wrong way round: it says the row comes first, whereas the signature of openpyxl 3.1.5 is Reference(worksheet, min_col, min_row, max_col, max_row), the column first, and Reference(sheet, 2, 1, 2, 10) is B1:B10; the chapter's example hides the mistake because it is a square block of cells, and leaving max_row out silently gives a single cell [P08] [P06] [P01]. The chapter's chart section also relies on import openpyxl alone making openpyxl.chart available, which holds in 3.1.5, so the call works as printed.", ["P08", "P06", "P07", "P01"]),
 ("F5", "Comparative analysis", "The cells that read data_only=True returns are the values that a spreadsheet application stored, never a calculation: a workbook that openpyxl wrote and saved gives None for every formula cell, and Project 10 loses the stored TOTAL of every row when it saves (D2 held 18.58 in the original file and None in the copy), whereas LibreOffice 24.2.7.2, run headless on the saved file, recalculated all 23,757 rows with 0 differences from the totals Excel had stored, and turned the chapter's =SUM(A1:A2) into the cached value 500 [P03] [P31] [P01]. XlsxWriter, which writes formulas, stores the value 0 as the result unless it is given another [P45]. The chapter's step of opening and saving the file in Excel is therefore the calculation, and a program cannot take its place with openpyxl alone.", ["P03", "P31", "P45", "P01", "P29"]),
 ("F6", "Contemporary developments", "A spreadsheet's ROUND and Python's round disagree on exact halves, a point that matters when a program is asked to check a total: on the real produce file Excel's stored =ROUND(B*C,2) differed from Python's round(price * pounds, 2) in 629 of 23,757 rows, and LibreOffice's recalculation of the updated file in 695 (for example 1.89 pounds times 34.5 is the exact half 65.205, which Excel stores as 65.21 and Python gives as 65.2, because the float 1.89 * 34.5 is held a little under 65.205) [P20] [P23] [P27] [P30]. Python rounds the binary number it holds, and a spreadsheet function rounds the decimal number it shows; a check that compares them must allow the difference or use decimal.", ["P20", "P23", "P27", "P30", "P01"]),
 ("F7", "Comparative analysis", "The font and size defaults the chapter states are only half true: Font(size=24, italic=True) writes no font name and Font(name='Times New Roman', bold=True) writes no size, because a style object keeps only what it is given, so the chapter's default of Calibri 11 holds only for cells that were never styled; LibreOffice 24.2 drew the cell with no name in Caladea-Italic, a substitute for Cambria, and the cell with the name Calibri in Carlito-Italic, as the fonts embedded in the PDF showed [P05] [P01] [P29]. Style objects are immutable (assigning cell.font.bold raises AttributeError), size='big' raises TypeError, and bold='yes' is accepted as true. Excel itself was not available to say what it draws.", ["P05", "P01", "P29"]),
 ("F8", "Comparative analysis", "Looking at a cell, a row or a column changes the workbook, a side effect the chapter does not mention: sheet.cell(row=50, column=2) creates the cell and raises max_row to 50, so Project 9's loop over range(2, sheet.max_row + 1) reaches empty rows and int(None) raises TypeError; reading row_dimensions[3] or column_dimensions['A'].width creates the dimension, which is saved as an empty row element or as a column of custom width 13 although nobody set it; and on the real census file the book's loop over cells peaked at 114 MB of traced memory while read_only iter_rows peaked at 11 MB for the same 72,864 rows [P04] [P09] [P01]. The sheet's size is what was touched, not what was typed.", ["P04", "P09", "P01"]),
 ("F9", "Comparative analysis", "Blank Row Inserter, one of the chapter's practice programs, is correct for values and incomplete for everything else: insert_rows moves the values and the styles of the cells below the new rows, but it does not rewrite the references inside formulas (a SUM over B1:B5 stays over B1:B5), does not move merged ranges (A5:B5 stayed) and does not move row heights (a height of 50 set on row 4 stayed on row 4 after two rows were inserted at row 3, and row 6 got none) [P04] [P01]. The chapter says nothing of this; the program must fix the rest itself, or the formulas will add the wrong cells.", ["P04", "P01"]),
 ("F10", "Comparative analysis", "Merged cells keep one real cell: after merge_cells('A1:D3') only A1 is a Cell and the others are MergedCell objects whose value is read-only (assigning to it raises AttributeError), the values the other cells held are discarded at once, and unmerge_cells gives back empty separate cells with the text in the top-left one [P04] [P01]. Freezing panes does not move the view: the book's produceSales3.xlsx was saved scrolled to topLeftCell A23736 and the copy frozen at A2 still opened at that row, so the frozen copy of Figure 14-8 shows the bottom of the data unless the program also resets the view [P04] [P28] [P01].", ["P04", "P28", "P01"]),
 ("F11", "Comparative analysis", "Four of the chapter's practice questions and the typeset listings need a note for an advanced course: Practice Question 10 asks for the string name of row 14, which does not exist because rows have numbers and only columns have letters; Question 16 asks how to hide a column, which the chapter never shows (sheet.column_dimensions['C'].hidden = True does it and the file records hidden=1); Question 11 has sheet['A1':'F1'] return a tuple of one tuple, not a flat tuple; and the five calls of Question 18 are Reference, Series, BarChart, append and add_chart. In the typeset Project 9 listing the call-out markers are inside the code: Step 1 as printed raises SyntaxError at the marker, and Step 2 compiles only when the marker is replaced by one space, not removed [P01] [P04] [P06].", ["P01", "P04", "P06"]),
 ("F12", "Contemporary developments", "openpyxl is current but its project is quiet: 3.1.5, uploaded on 2024-06-28, is the newest stable release on 2026-10-12 (the first upload to PyPI was on 2010-08-11), the classifiers of the wheel go only to Python 3.11 and Requires-Python is >=3.8, the stable documentation still names version 3.1.3 of 2024-05-29, and it installs and runs the same on Python 3.12, 3.13 and every release of 3.14 from 3.14.0 to 3.14.6; its only dependency is et-xmlfile [P11] [P10] [P02] [P12] [P19]. A course that teaches openpyxl is teaching a stable library that is no longer changing quickly, and should say which release it ran.", ["P11", "P10", "P02", "P12", "P19", "P40", "P38", "P39"]),
 ("F13", "Contemporary developments", "The security note of the openpyxl page matters because an .xlsx file is a zip of XML parts and the chapter opens files it did not make: the page says openpyxl does not guard against quadratic blowup or billion laughs and advises installing defusedxml [P02]. Run on Python 3.14.6, whose bundled Expat is 2.8.1, a 4.9 KB workbook with a nested entity of depth 4 loaded into 100,000 characters in a cell, a depth of 6 (ten million characters) was refused by Expat with a limit on input amplification, and with defusedxml installed load_workbook refused both with ValueError [P22] [P32] [P33]. The Python documentation says Expat versions below 2.7.2 may still be vulnerable, so the protection depends on the library the interpreter was built with.", ["P02", "P22", "P32", "P33"]),
 ("F14", "Conclusion", "For an advanced course, chapter 14 is best taught as a study of what openpyxl keeps of what a spreadsheet application stored: the values and formulas are kept, the results of formulas, the draw of fonts and charts and the window state are not computed or drawn by Python; the library creates what is looked at, loses cached results on save, does not follow references when it moves cells, and trusts the file it reads [P02] [P03] [P04]. The chapter's examples are sound, its projects run on the real files, and its two shipped scripts, one misnamed argument order, three figure descriptions, two practice questions and a few typeset marks are the corrections to teach; XlsxWriter, xlrd, pandas and the Office Open XML standard are the ecosystem around it [P35] [P36] [P37] [P44].", ["P01", "P02", "P03", "P04", "P35", "P36", "P37", "P44", "P26"]),
]

def X(code):
    """An executable example as one expression with no double quote and no line break: the code runs in a fresh
    namespace and the value of its variable out is the result."""
    lit = "'" + code.replace("\\", "\\\\").replace("'", "\\'").replace("\n", "\\n") + "'"
    return "(lambda g: (exec(%s, g), g['out'])[1])({})" % lit

